  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is an extrusion die?",
      "a": "A die is a thick steel plate with an opening shaped like the cross-section you want. Whatever is pushed through comes out with that shape, so changing the die changes the product.",
      "asks": [
        "What decides the shape in extrusion?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between extrusion and injection moulding?",
      "a": "Extrusion pushes material continuously through a shaped hole to make long products with the same cross-section. Injection moulding fills a closed mould to make separate, fully shaped parts like caps or toys.",
      "asks": [
        "Extrusion or moulding?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What things are made by extrusion?",
      "a": "Pipes, hoses, window and door frames, drinking straws, plastic sheet and film, cable insulation, and aluminium rails and frames are all made by extrusion. Even pasta and some snacks are extruded.",
      "asks": [
        "Is pasta extruded?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can metal be extruded?",
      "a": "Yes. A hot block of aluminium, at roughly 450 to 500 degrees Celsius, is soft enough to be pushed through a steel die by a powerful ram while still solid, making frames, rails and heat sinks.",
      "asks": [
        "How are aluminium frames made?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How long can an extruded pipe be?",
      "a": "As long as you like, because extrusion is continuous. Pipes are cut into lengths or, if flexible, wound onto huge reels hundreds of metres long.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are some pipes sold on reels?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Joseph Bramah patented an early extrusion process for making lead pipe in 1797.",
    "Aluminium is usually extruded as a solid at around 450 to 500 °C, well below its melting point of 660 °C."
  ],
